+# Product Requirements Document (PRD)
+
+**Project**: Pulseboard
+**Version**: 1.0.0
+**Date**: 2026-08-11
+**Status**: Planning
+**Scale**: Level 2
+
+---
+
+## Information Sources
+
+**Traceability**: This plan is derived only from the supplied project foundation.
+
+- `project.md` — product purpose, pilot outcome, and non-goals.
+- `product-contract.md` — PRM-001 through PRM-004.
+- `evidence-contract.md` — required end-to-end, isolation, and facilitator-LARP evidence.
+- `context.md` — four-week, two-engineer pilot; Python API; browser UI; unselected reminder provider.
+
+`architecture.md`, `ux-strategy.md`, research reports, and brownfield artifacts were not supplied. At Build level these are optional inputs. This plan therefore keeps the architecture to a reversible boundary assumption: a Python API serves a browser UI, and reminder delivery is behind a provider adapter.
+
+**Information flow**:
+
+```
+project.md + product-contract.md + evidence-contract.md + context.md
+  ↓
+PRD.md + epics.md
+  ↓
+E000 foundation → E001 reviewed actions → E002 reminders and delivery status
+```
+
+---
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+Pulseboard is a four-week pilot for meeting facilitators. They upload an existing meeting transcript, review proposed action items, explicitly confirm each item’s text, owner, and due date, then schedule one reminder through a configurable provider. The product must prevent cross-workspace access to transcripts and actions. It deliberately does not record meetings, replace calendars, or send autonomous outbound messages.
+
+## Product Vision & Strategy
+
+### Vision Statement
+
+Give facilitators a dependable handoff from an already-held meeting to owned, due-dated actions, without asking them to trust an autonomous system with commitments.
+
+### Strategic Objectives
+
+1. Turn transcript review into confirmed actions: satisfy PRM-001 and PRM-002 without bypassing facilitator judgment.
+2. Make confirmed actions operational: satisfy PRM-003 with one observable reminder attempt and delivery status.
+3. Earn pilot trust: satisfy PRM-004 and the evidence contract’s real-role isolation probe.
+
+### Success Definition
+
+- Pilot success: five teams use the pilot.
+- Review success: facilitators review at least 80% of proposed action items.
+- Outcome success: unowned overdue actions fall by 30% over four weeks.
+- Product safety: no action is scheduled before a facilitator has explicitly confirmed its text, owner, and due date.
+
+## Market Analysis
+
+### Target Market
+
+The supplied evidence defines a pilot market, not a quantified market: teams that run meetings and have a facilitator responsible for turning discussion into accountable follow-up. No TAM, SAM, SOM, competitor, or pricing evidence is present; none is asserted here.
+
+### Market Opportunity
+
+The supported opportunity is the gap between a meeting transcript and a reviewed, owned, due-dated action. Pulseboard narrows that gap while preserving human control over every commitment.
+
+## User Analysis
+
+### User Personas
+
+**Meeting facilitator**
+
+- **Role**: uploads a transcript and decides which proposed actions become commitments.
+- **Goal**: finish follow-up with clear owners and dates.
+- **Pain point**: action items can remain vague, unowned, or overdue after a meeting.
+- **Success**: each scheduled action was reviewed and explicitly confirmed.
+
+**Action owner**
+
+- **Role**: receives the single configured reminder for a confirmed action.
+- **Goal**: know what they own and by when.
+- **Success**: can rely on a reminder whose delivery status is visible to the facilitator.
+
+### User Journey
+
+1. A facilitator has an existing transcript from a completed meeting.
+2. The facilitator uploads it and reviews proposed action items.
+3. The facilitator confirms or corrects each item’s text, owner, and due date.
+4. Pulseboard schedules one reminder only for a fully confirmed item.
+5. The facilitator can see the reminder’s delivery status and follow up when delivery fails.
+
+## Functional Requirements
+
+### Core Features
+
+#### Reviewed action creation
+
+**Transcript upload and proposal extraction (PRM-001)**
+
+- **Description**: A facilitator uploads a transcript and receives proposed action items for review.
+- **Acceptance criteria**:
+  - A workspace-scoped transcript can be uploaded through the browser UI.
+  - The system produces proposed action items associated with that transcript and workspace.
+  - A proposal is not a scheduled action.
+- **Priority**: P0
+
+**Explicit confirmation gate (PRM-002)**
+
+- **Description**: A facilitator must explicitly confirm the final text, owner, and due date before an item can be scheduled.
+- **Acceptance criteria**:
+  - Confirmation requires all three fields: text, owner, and due date.
+  - The facilitator may correct a proposal before confirmation.
+  - Attempts to schedule an unconfirmed or incomplete item are rejected.
+- **Priority**: P0
+
+#### Reminder delivery
+
+**Configurable provider delivery (PRM-003)**
+
+- **Description**: A confirmed action schedules one reminder through a selected configurable provider.
+- **Acceptance criteria**:
+  - Delivery is attempted only after the confirmation gate succeeds.
+  - The selected provider is configurable behind an adapter boundary.
+  - The facilitator can see a delivery status for the one reminder attempt.
+- **Priority**: P0
+
+#### Workspace isolation
+
+**Tenant boundary (PRM-004)**
+
+- **Description**: A workspace cannot read another workspace’s transcripts or actions.
+- **Acceptance criteria**:
+  - Transcript and action reads are scoped to the requesting workspace.
+  - A real request-role negative probe for a different workspace is denied.
+- **Priority**: P0
+
+### Platform Requirements
+
+- **Web**: browser UI for facilitators; exact browser support is not supplied and remains a later implementation decision.
+- **API**: Python API serving the browser UI.
+- **Excluded**: native mobile and desktop applications are outside the supplied scope.
+
+### Integration Requirements
+
+| System | Type | Purpose | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Reminder provider | Configurable outbound adapter | Send one reminder and report its delivery status | P0 |
+
+The provider is intentionally not selected in the supplied foundation. Selecting one is deferred to implementation planning; the adapter boundary keeps that choice reversible.
+
+## Non-Functional Requirements
+
+### Reliability and observability
+
+- Preserve a delivery status for each configured reminder attempt.
+- E000 establishes local lint, test, and build commands so the pilot path can be checked repeatedly.
+- The runtime evidence path is upload → review/confirmation → delivery status.
+
+### Security and privacy
+
+- Enforce workspace-scoped access to transcripts and actions.
+- Prove isolation with a negative probe using the real request role, not a mock or privileged path.
+- The supplied context states that no regulated health or payment data is in scope; this is not a compliance exemption for workspace isolation.
+
+### Usability
+
+- The facilitator’s review and confirmation step must be legible enough for a facilitator LARP.
+- The UI must distinguish a proposed item from a confirmed, scheduled action.
+- No outbound message is sent until all required confirmation fields are explicit.
+
+### Performance and scalability
+
+No numeric response-time, volume, availability, or growth targets are supplied. The four-week pilot and five-team success criterion bound this first plan; performance budgets must be set before a scale claim is made.
+
+## Design Principles
+
+### User Experience Principles
+
+1. **Human commitment gate**: proposal generation may assist, but a human must own the final text, owner, and due date.
+2. **Status is visible**: a facilitator can distinguish proposed, confirmed, and delivery-status states.
+3. **Scope restraint**: do not add recording, calendar replacement, or autonomous outbound messaging.
+
+### Technical Principles
+
+1. **Workspace isolation by design**: every transcript and action access path carries and enforces workspace scope.
+2. **Provider boundary**: reminder-specific code depends on a configurable adapter, not a permanently assumed provider.
+3. **Evidence-bearing delivery path**: implementation must support the evidence contract’s real end-to-end and negative-role probes.
+
+## Constraints & Assumptions
+
+### Business Constraints
+
+- **Timeline**: four-week pilot.
+- **Resources**: two engineers.
+- **Pilot target**: five teams.
+- **Non-goals**: meeting recording, calendar replacement, and autonomous outbound messaging.
+
+### Technical Constraints
+
+- Python API and browser UI.
+- Reminder provider not selected.
+- No regulated health or payment data in the pilot scope.
+
+### Assumptions
+
+1. **Existing transcripts are supplied by the facilitator**: recording or transcription ingestion is not planned; if wrong, scope must be adjusted before implementation.
+2. **A single reminder is sufficient for the pilot**: recurring reminders and escalation are out of scope; if wrong, PRM-003 needs expansion.
+3. **Workspace identity is available to the request path**: if it is not, E001 must establish an authentication/tenant context before transcript access can be safely implemented.
+
+## Release Strategy
+
+### MVP Scope
+
+The MVP is the evidence-contract path: a facilitator uploads a transcript, reviews and explicitly confirms an action, schedules one reminder through a configurable provider, and sees delivery status while workspace isolation holds.
+
+#### MVP Features
+
+- E000 developer infrastructure and testable pilot baseline.
+- E001 transcript proposals, review, confirmation, and workspace isolation.
+- E002 configured reminder delivery and visible status.
+
+#### MVP Success Criteria
+
+- Five pilot teams can use the full facilitator workflow.
+- At least 80% of proposed action items are reviewed.
+- Unowned overdue actions decrease by 30% over four weeks.
+- Required runtime end-to-end, isolation, and facilitator-LARP evidence exists before any readiness claim.
+
+### Release Phases
+
+**Phase 0: Foundation**
+
+- Establish developer infrastructure and the executable evidence baseline.
+
+**Phase 1: Reviewed actions**
+
+- Deliver workspace-scoped transcript upload, proposal extraction, and the explicit confirmation gate.
+
+**Phase 2: Reminder completion**
+
+- Connect confirmed actions to the configured reminder provider and expose delivery status.
+
+### Rollout Strategy
+
+- **Pilot**: begin with the five-team cohort named by the project goal.
+- **Feature rollout**: the confirmation gate is mandatory for all pilot use; no autonomous-send mode is introduced.
+- **Rollback**: disable reminder scheduling while retaining reviewable action records if the provider path is unsafe or unreliable.
+
+## Success Metrics
+
+| Area | Metric | Target | Measurement |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| Pilot adoption | Participating teams | 5 | Pilot roster / workspace count |
+| Review behavior | Proposed items reviewed | 80% | Proposal and review records |
+| Outcome | Reduction in unowned overdue actions | 30% in four weeks | Action ownership and due-date records |
+| Delivery | Confirmed reminder attempts with visible status | 100% | Delivery status records |
+| Isolation | Cross-workspace negative probe succeeds | 100% of release probes | Real request-role evidence |
+
+## Risk Analysis
+
+| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Proposals are inaccurate or ambiguous | Medium | High | Require facilitator correction and explicit confirmation | E001 |
+| A reminder provider choice delays delivery work | Medium | Medium | Use a configurable adapter; defer provider selection without blocking review flow | E002 |
+| Workspace boundary is implemented only in UI | Medium | High | Test a real request-role cross-workspace denial | E001 |
+| Four-week pilot scope expands into calendar or recording work | Medium | High | Keep stated non-goals out of epics and acceptance criteria | Project owner |
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- E001 depends on E000’s test and environment baseline.
+- E002 depends on confirmed actions from E001.
+- Provider selection is a dependency of E002 implementation, but no provider is assumed in this plan.
+- The evidence contract governs final runtime proof; this planning phase does not claim validated readiness.
